---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>Traverse puts two enforcement points between an AI agent and your business logic today: schema-based discovery, and input/output schema validation around execution. Each one catches a different class of error before it can propagate. (A contract can also declare <code>preconditions</code> and <code>postconditions</code>, but as of v0.1 those are documentation for reviewers, not a runtime-enforced third gate — put anything you need actually enforced into the JSON Schema itself.)</p>\n\n      <h2>Accurate specs at discovery</h2>\n      <p>The first source of agent errors is a mismatch between what the agent thinks a tool does and what it actually does. Traverse eliminates this by surfacing the full JSON Schema for every capability through the MCP stdio server's <code>describe_entrypoint</code> command. The agent does not build assumptions from a description — it reads the schema. Required fields, types, value ranges: all machine-readable and authoritative.</p>\n\n      <h2>Input validation before execution</h2>\n      <p>Agents sometimes construct inputs that technically match the type but should still be rejected — a quantity of zero, a negative price. Declaring these as constraints in the input schema itself (a <code>minimum</code>, an <code>enum</code>, a <code>format</code>) means the runtime checks them before the WASM binary runs and returns <code>request_invalid</code> with the failing field and value. The agent can read this and self-correct.</p>\n\n      <h2>Output validation after execution</h2>\n      <p>A buggy capability could return an output that violates its own contract. Maybe a pricing function returns a negative total under an edge case. If the output schema declares that total must be non-negative, output validation catches this before the result reaches the agent, returning <code>output_validation_failed</code> instead. The agent never reasons on top of a result the schema doesn't allow.</p>\n\n      <h2>Structured errors the agent can act on</h2>\n      <p>Both enforcement points return a structured error with a stable code and details through the MCP server. This is more useful than an HTTP 400 with a message string. A well-designed agent loop can read the error, adjust the call, and retry without human intervention.</p>\n\n      <p>See <a href=\"/questions/how-does-traverse-handle-errors.html\">how Traverse handles errors</a> for the full error type reference.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How does Traverse prevent AI agent errors?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Traverse prevents agent errors at two enforced points: schema-based discovery gives the agent accurate tool specs, and input/output JSON Schema validation around execution rejects bad inputs (request_invalid) and bad outputs (output_validation_failed) with a structured error the agent can act on. Contract-declared preconditions/postconditions are documentation today, not a separate runtime-enforced gate.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"How does Traverse prevent AI agent errors?"}
  description={"Traverse stops AI agents from calling capabilities with wrong inputs, and prevents bad outputs from reaching the agent, through schema-based discovery and input/output validation. Learn the enforcement points."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-does-traverse-prevent-ai-agent-errors.html"}
  category={"AI and MCP"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
